form of AS. ''bord'', a plank, appears transposed in AS. ''bred'', a board, flat surface, E. dial. ''brede'', a board, = OD. ''bred'', D. ''berd'', a floor, = OHG. MHG. ''bret'', G. ''brett'', a board, plank, = Sw. ''bräde'' = Dan. ''brædt'', board. Not connected with ''broad'', as is usually supposed. Cf. Ir. Gael. Corn. ''bord'' = W. ''bord'' and ''bwrdd'', a board, table.] '''1.''' A piece of timber sawed thin, and of considerable length and breadth compared with the thickness. {{smaller|The name is usually given to pieces of timber (in this and similar forms called ''lumber'' in the United States) more than 4½ inches wide and less than 2 inches thick. Thicker pieces of the same form are called ''planks'', and narrower ones ''battens''. When boards are thinner on one edge than on the other, they are called ''feather-edged boards;'' and to riven pieces of this kind, not more than 3 feet long, used for roofing, the name ''board'' is exclusively applied in the southern United States.}}
{{smaller block|But ships are but ''boards'', sailors but men.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' A table, especially as being used to place food on.
{{smaller block|<poem>Fruit of all kinds . . .
She gathers, tribute large, and on the ''board''
Heaps with unsparing hand. ''Milton'', P. L., v. 343.</poem>}}
Hence—'''3.''' (''a'') That which is served on a board or table; entertainment; food; diet.
{{smaller block|<poem>Sometimes white lilies did their leaves afford,
With wholesome poppy-flowers, to mend his homely ''board''.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Virgil’s Georgics, iv.}}}}
{{smaller block|They . . . suffer from cold and hunger in their fireless houses and at their meagre ''boards''.
{{right|''Howells'', Venetian Life, xxi.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
(''b'') Provision for a person’s daily meals, or food and lodging, especially as furnished by agreement or for a price: applied also to the like provision for horses and other animals.
{{smaller block|Board without lodging is often distinguished either as ''day-board'' or ''table-board''.}}

'''4.''' A table at which a council or the session of a tribunal is held.
{{smaller block|I wish the king would be pleased sometimes to be present at that ''board''; it adds a majesty to it.
{{right|''Bacon.''}}
Better acquainted with affairs than any other who sat then at that ''board''.
{{right|''Clarendon.''}}}}

Hence, by metonymy—'''5.''' A number of persons having the management, direction, or superintendence of some public or private office or trust: as, a ''board'' of directors; the ''board'' of trade; the ''board'' of health; a school-''board''.
{{smaller block|The honourable ''board'' of council.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. VIII., i. 1.}}
''Boards'' partake of a part of the inconveniences of larger assemblies. Their decisions are slower, their energy less, their responsibility more diffused. They will not have the same abilities and knowledge as an administration by single men.
{{right|''A. Hamilton'', Works, I. 154.}}}}

'''6.''' A flat slab of wood used for some specific purpose: as, an ironing-''board''; a bake-''board'';

