¶
{{smaller block|If after “stoning out” the leather should require softening, it is ''boarded''.{{right|''C. T. Davis'', Leather, p. 431.}}}}

'''3.''' To place at board: as, he ''boarded'' his son with Mrs. So-and-so.—'''4.''' To furnish with food, or food and lodging, for a compensation: as, his landlady ''boards'' him at a reasonable price.

{{smaller block|He was . . . ''boarded'' and lodged at the houses of the farmers whose children he instructed.{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 421.}}}}

'''5.''' To come up alongside of (in order to attack); fall aboard of.—'''6.''' To go on board of (a vessel). {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') To embark. (''b'') To hail and enter officially, as a custom-house or other officer. (''c'') To enter by force, or in a hostile manner.}}

{{smaller block|You ''board'' an enemy to capture her, and a stranger to receive news or make communications.{{right|''Totten.''}}}}

'''7†.''' To put on board; stow away.

{{smaller block|The seamen call; shall we ''board'' your trunks?{{right|''Middleton and Rowley'', Changeling, i. 1.}}}}

'''8†.''' To approach; accost; make advances to.

{{smaller block|<poem>Him the Prince with gentle court did ''bord''.</poem>{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. ix. 2.}}}}

